  "notes": [
    "Not 5 V tolerant. The pins take 0 to 3.3 V and nothing more, which catches people moving over from an Uno or a Mega.",
    "600 MHz with 1 MB of RAM, which is roughly thirty times an Arduino Due and in a smaller package. The Audio Library is the usual reason to pick one.",
    "Seven hardware serial ports, three SPI and three I2C. Serial1 is on pins 0 and 1, Serial2 on 7 and 8, Serial3 on 15 and 14, Serial4 on 16 and 17, Serial5 on 21 and 20.",
    "Wire is on pins 18 and 19, Wire1 on 17 and 16. The second I2C bus shares pins with Serial4, so you get one or the other.",
    "Pin 13 is the on-board LED and the SPI clock at once. PJRC warn against INPUT_PULLUP on it.",
    "Vin accepts 3.6 to 5.5 V, and the 3.3 V output is good for 250 mA. To run from an external supply with USB also plugged in, cut the VUSB pad on the underside first.",
    "Another sixteen pads sit on the underside for pins 24 to 39, plus five on the back edge for program, on/off, ground and VBat. Those are not on this diagram.",
    "Every digital pin can raise an interrupt and 31 of them do PWM, so the usual hunt for a free interrupt or timer pin does not apply here.",
    "The Teensy 4.1 is the longer version of the same chip, adding Ethernet, a microSD slot, a USB host port and 55 I/O."
  ],
